WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.310
Imagine two colleagues who are both new to reviewing grant

2
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:07.290
agreements with community organizations. One has a background in finance.

3
00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.150
The other recently came from front line work in a

4
00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.850
county office, and speaks two languages that several grantees use.

5
00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.950
Their supervisor has to decide how to support each of

6
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:17.110
them.

7
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.820
On paper, one looks more ready than the other. But

8
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.840
I suspect the page is going to tell us that

9
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.720
paper is not the whole story. What is adaptive and

10
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.620
situational leadership?

11
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.450
It is less a single style than a discipline for

12
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.670
choosing among styles. Situational leadership, from Paul Hersey and Ken

13
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.750
Blanchard, matches the amount of direction and support to each

14
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.130
person's skill and confidence on a specific task.

15
00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.090
Adaptive leadership, from Ronald Heifetz and Marty Linsky, looks at

16
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.550
the problem itself, and asks whether it is technical, with

17
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.790
a known fix, or adaptive, which requires people to change

18
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:52.830
habits and assumptions.

19
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.840
So the leader is reading two things at once: the

20
00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.060
person, and the kind of problem in front of them.

21
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.760
That sounds demanding, but also very realistic about how work

22
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.360
actually goes.

23
00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:07.210
It is both. An adaptive leader also resists giving quick

24
00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.410
answers to complex problems. Instead, they hold the team in

25
00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.450
productive discomfort long enough to learn, test ideas and build

26
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.470
a solution together, while keeping the pressure manageable so people

27
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.670
do not panic or shut down.

28
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.560
What would I notice about a leader who works this

29
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.480
way? How would I know they are adapting on purpose

30
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:24.680
rather than just being unpredictable?

31
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.650
They pause to ask whether a problem is a known

32
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.990
fix or a deeper challenge. They explain out loud when

33
00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.650
they shift from collaborative to directive, so it feels purposeful.

34
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.290
Support is calibrated by task, not by person: an experienced

35
00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.970
colleague learning new software gets structure, while a newer colleague

36
00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.790
doing something they have mastered gets room.

37
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.670
And on unscripted problems, they run small pilots and adjust

38
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:50.110
quickly.

39
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.600
Where does this approach shine? Public service seems full of

40
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.040
changing rules, funding and community needs.

41
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.950
Exactly there. In volatile, layered work, where laws, budgets and

42
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.210
staffing shift constantly, it meets people where they are instead

43
00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.990
of forcing every person and every problem into one mold.

44
00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.370
It also saves money and effort, because leaders stop buying

45
00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.230
technical fixes, like new software, for problems that are really

46
00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.410
about trust or habits.

47
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.620
It also sounds hard to do well. Where does the

48
00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.260
page say adaptive leadership tends to go wrong in practice?

49
00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.510
It demands strong self awareness and the discipline to act

50
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.410
outside your comfort zone. Without explanation, style shifts feel like

51
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:34.750
whiplash. And the most common failure is misdiagnosis: treating a

52
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.010
deep challenge, like rebuilding community trust, as a checklist, or

53
00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.030
turning a simple technical fix, like a broken payroll system,

54
00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.830
into a long philosophical debate.

55
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:47.120
Let's talk about fair service and staff. The page says

56
00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.080
this style is highly effective for equity. Why is that?

57
00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:55.310
Because equity is the classic adaptive challenge. Some parts are

58
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.590
technical, like fixing a document so screen readers can use

59
00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:03.050
it. The core work is adaptive: changing mental models and

60
00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:03.750
long held habits.

61
00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:09.130
Situational leadership also reinforces fairness over sameness, by giving each

62
00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.950
person the support they actually need, which fits naturally with

63
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:16.410
accommodations, different learning styles and different career stages.

64
00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.300
And the risk? I am thinking about our two colleagues

65
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.640
again, and how quickly one of them might be judged

66
00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.340
less ready.

67
00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:27.210
That is the risk. Situational leadership depends on the leader's

68
00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:31.270
judgment of readiness, and unexamined assumptions can warp it. An

69
00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:35.170
accent, a quieter cultural style or neurodivergent communication can be

70
00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:39.190
misread as low readiness, keeping someone in close supervision indefinitely.

71
00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.090
A second risk is using patience as a reason to

72
00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.270
delay urgent fixes, like an accommodation or an inaccessible public

73
00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:46.790
website.

74
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.840
So what should the supervisor do with these two new

75
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.540
grant reviewers? Splitting the work by background seems tempting, but

76
00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.220
probably wrong.

77
00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.570
Ask each of them what they know, what they find

78
00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.250
hard and what support they would like, then pair them

79
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:04.010
so each can teach the other. Support fits their real

80
00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.610
readiness rather than assumptions, and both kinds of expertise improve

81
00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:11.410
the reviews. Giving the finance colleague the complex agreements by

82
00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.350
default could hold back someone whose strengths simply look different.

83
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.140
That feels like the heart of this style: ask, do

84
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.380
not assume. What else does the page suggest leaders try?

85
00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.730
Fix technical accessibility and compliance barriers immediately, and save patient

86
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:31.350
dialogue for the deeper cultural shifts. Base decisions about support

87
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.410
on documented experience and a direct question to the person,

88
00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.110
such as what level of support would help most here.

89
00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.010
And narrate your style shifts, so everyone experiences fairness and

90
00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:41.490
predictability.

91
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.180
Here's a question to carry with you. When do you

92
00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.140
assume someone is ready, and when do you ask them?

93
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:49.480
Thank you for listening.
